WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:28.960
SpaceX will attempt the first orbital flight of its Starship rocket today, a test that could open new routes for satellites and future crewed missions beyond Earth orbit. This is Omni View, episode 189. SpaceX plans to launch the Starship rocket from Texas on a roughly 10-hour test that aims to place the upper stage in Earth orbit and deploy 26 Starlink satellites. The vehicle has undergone multiple prior tests, most of which ended in explosions.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:35.120
and this flight would mark the first attempt at a full orbital trajectory after three years of development.

00:00:35.660 --> 00:00:39.500
Success would advance plans for refueling tests and longer missions.

00:00:40.220 --> 00:00:46.840
The launch is scheduled to begin at 7.15 a.m. local time and forms the 14th test flight overall,

00:00:47.340 --> 00:00:50.440
coming after a June initial public offering by the company.

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:55.980
France 24 reports the launch window opens at 7.15 a.m. local time

00:00:55.980 --> 00:01:04.060
and notes that the rocket must perform a second burn to reach the target circular orbit at about 275 kilometers altitude.

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:12.120
The outlet emphasizes the technical demands of operating under varying gravity levels and the history of earlier suborbital flights.

00:01:12.780 --> 00:01:19.740
SpaceX has described the goal as ushering in an exciting new phase that includes orbital refueling demonstrations.

00:01:19.740 --> 00:01:25.520
Cathleen Curley of Georgetown University's Center for Security and Emerging Technology

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:32.000
told AFP that reaching the required speed of roughly 28,000 kilometers per hour remains

00:01:32.000 --> 00:01:33.660
the hardest part of the profile.

00:01:34.200 --> 00:01:39.360
The black and silver vehicle, described as the largest and most powerful object ever flown,

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:45.240
has received numerous modifications since its first full configuration flights began in 2023.

